// IAM Role Trust-Policy Backdoor (T1098.001)
//
// Two signals: a role trust policy rewritten to trust a principal outside the
// organisation with no confining Condition, and that trust then being exercised — a
// cross-account or federated AssumeRole landing in this account's own trail.
//
// This query does what the Sigma rules structurally cannot. A trust policy is decided by
// three things read together from the SAME statement: the Effect, the Principal, and
// whether a Condition confines it. Substring matching answers none of them, so the Sigma
// file approximates and this parses. Everything below the parse is exact.
//
// The key discriminator is the Condition, not the principal. A trust naming a foreign
// 12-digit account and carrying `sts:ExternalId` is the documented third-party-access
// pattern; the identical trust without it is a backdoor. A rule that alerts on the
// principal alone pages on every partner integration and gets muted.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL. NOT CloudWatch Logs Insights.
//
// DEPLOYMENT PARAMETERS — the two lets below. They are the same lists the Sigma rules
// carry. Generate OrgAccounts from Organizations rather than maintaining it by hand.
let OrgAccounts   = dynamic(["<this-account-id>", "<other-org-account-id>"]);
let KnownIdPRegex = @"(oidc-provider/<your-idp-host>|saml-provider/<YourIdPName>|cognito-identity\.amazonaws\.com)";
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where EventSource == "iam.amazonaws.com"
| where EventName in ("UpdateAssumeRolePolicy", "CreateRole")
| where isempty(ErrorCode)
| extend Req  = parse_json(RequestParameters)
| extend Resp = parse_json(ResponseElements)
| extend RoleName = tostring(Req.roleName)
//
// THE FIELD SPLIT. UpdateAssumeRolePolicy carries the trust document as `policyDocument`;
// CreateRole carries it as `assumeRolePolicyDocument`. They never co-occur on one event,
// so coalesce reads whichever is present. Testing for both on one event would match
// neither.
| extend DocRaw = tostring(coalesce(Req.policyDocument, Req.assumeRolePolicyDocument))
//
// There is no size-evasion branch here, and its absence is deliberate. CloudTrail omits
// requestParameters only above 100 KB; a role trust policy is capped at 2,048 characters
// by default and 8,192 at the adjustable maximum — twelve times below that threshold. The
// 131,072 in the API reference is the parameter LENGTH CONSTRAINT, wire-level input
// validation, not the enforced quota: a document between the two passes validation and is
// then refused with LimitExceeded. It is never stored, never enforced, and never produces
// a success event, so an empty DocRaw on a successful write is a malformed pipeline, not
// an evasion. Do not build a verdict on it.
//
// THE DECODE, conditional and in the right direction. requestParameters is RAW JSON;
// percent-encoding is a property of what IAM RETURNS. Decoding unconditionally corrupts a
// raw document that legitimately contains a "%" or a "+". This handles a response-side
// document too, which is what makes the same query reusable against ResponseElements.
| extend Doc   = parse_json(iff(DocRaw startswith "%", url_decode(DocRaw), DocRaw))
//
// SHAPE GUARD, level 1. IAM permits Statement as a single object OR an array. mv-expand
// over a bare object expands its PROPERTIES, so the statement is silently skipped and the
// sweep reports clean on exactly the trust it exists to find.
| extend Stmts = iff(gettype(Doc.Statement) == "array", Doc.Statement, pack_array(Doc.Statement))
| mv-expand Stmt = Stmts
| extend Effect = tostring(Stmt.Effect)
| where Effect =~ "Allow"
//
// SHAPE GUARD, level 2. Principal is an OBJECT or the bare string "*" — documented as
// equivalent to {"AWS":"*"} — and Principal.AWS / .Federated / .Service are each a string
// OR an array. Three shapes, normalised to arrays before anything is tested.
| extend P = Stmt.Principal
| extend AwsP = case(
      gettype(P) == "string",         pack_array(tostring(P)),
      gettype(P.AWS) == "array",      todynamic(tostring(P.AWS)),
      isnotempty(tostring(P.AWS)),    pack_array(tostring(P.AWS)),
      dynamic([]))
| extend FedP = case(
      gettype(P.Federated) == "array",      todynamic(tostring(P.Federated)),
      isnotempty(tostring(P.Federated)),    pack_array(tostring(P.Federated)),
      dynamic([]))
| extend SvcP = case(
      gettype(P.Service) == "array",        todynamic(tostring(P.Service)),
      isnotempty(tostring(P.Service)),      pack_array(tostring(P.Service)),
      dynamic([]))
//
// NO NotPrincipal BRANCH, and the reason is the whole method in miniature. A role trust
// policy cannot carry NotPrincipal at all: AWS documents that it "must be used with
// "Effect":"Deny"" AND that "You cannot use the NotPrincipal element in an IAM
// identity-based policy nor in an IAM role trust policy", and IAM Access Analyzer
// publishes the rejection as ROLE_TRUST_POLICY_SYNTAX_ERROR_NOTPRINCIPAL. The write fails
// with MalformedPolicyDocument, which sets ErrorCode, which `isempty(ErrorCode)` above
// already excluded. A verdict here would have been dead twice over — rejected shape,
// filtered event — and would have sent a responder looking for a stored document that was
// never written. The attempt IS evidence; see the ATTEMPTED-AND-REJECTED companion below.
//
// `set_has_element`, never `strcat_array` plus equality: strcat_array strips the quotes,
// so a test for a quoted asterisk is dead code and an equality test drops
// ["123456789012","*"] — a multi-element array whose second element is the whole problem.
// FedP is not tested for "*": "Federated":"*" is rejected too
// (INVALID_FEDERATED_PRINCIPAL_SYNTAX_IN_ROLE_TRUST_POLICY — "Update the federated
// principal to a domain name or a SAML metadata ARN"). {"AWS":"*"} is the one wildcard
// form AWS documents as accepted in a trust policy, and the bare "Principal":"*" is not
// (ROLE_TRUST_POLICY_UNSUPPORTED_WILDCARD_IN_PRINCIPAL) — but AwsP normalises the bare
// string anyway, at no cost, because the same shape guard serves other resource policies.
| extend WildcardPrincipal = set_has_element(AwsP, "*")
//
// `contains` throughout, never `has`. Every operand here is punctuated — "sts:ExternalId",
// ":sub", "arn:aws:iam::" — and `has` is whole-term matching that breaks on all of them.
| extend AwsStr = tostring(AwsP)
| extend FedStr = tostring(FedP)
// RE2 has no lookbehind; \b is the portable 12-digit boundary. extract_all returns NULL
// when nothing matches — not an empty array — so it is coalesced before set_difference
// and array_length see it. A null there propagates through both and silently disposes a
// statement as clean.
| extend AccountsFound    = coalesce(extract_all(@"\b(\d{12})\b", AwsStr), dynamic([]))
| extend ExternalAccounts = set_difference(AccountsFound, OrgAccounts)
| extend UnknownFederated = array_length(FedP) > 0 and not(FedStr matches regex KnownIdPRegex)
//
// THE DISCRIMINATOR. A Condition that names any of these narrows who may actually assume
// the role. sts:ExternalId addresses the confused-deputy problem; aws:PrincipalOrgID binds
// the caller to an Organizations membership; a pinned OIDC subject or audience binds a
// federated trust to one workload. Their presence is what separates a partner integration
// from a backdoor.
| extend CondStr  = tostring(Stmt.Condition)
| extend Confined = isnotempty(CondStr) and (
       CondStr contains "sts:ExternalId"
    or CondStr contains "aws:PrincipalOrgID"
    or CondStr contains "aws:PrincipalOrgPaths"
    or CondStr contains "aws:PrincipalArn"
    or CondStr contains "aws:PrincipalAccount"
    or CondStr contains "aws:SourceAccount"
    or CondStr contains "aws:SourceArn"
    or CondStr contains "aws:SourceIp"
    or CondStr contains "aws:SourceVpce"
    or CondStr contains ":sub"
    or CondStr contains ":aud")
| extend Verdict = case(
    WildcardPrincipal and not(Confined),
        "PUBLIC ROLE — any AWS principal may assume it, no confining Condition, P0",
    array_length(ExternalAccounts) > 0 and not(Confined),
        "EXTERNAL ACCOUNT TRUSTED with no confining Condition — persistence that survives credential rotation, P0",
    UnknownFederated and not(Confined),
        "UNRECOGNISED FEDERATED PROVIDER, token not pinned — the provider decides who gets in, P0",
    WildcardPrincipal or array_length(ExternalAccounts) > 0 or UnknownFederated,
        "OUTSIDE PRINCIPAL, Condition-bound — disposition it, do not act, P3",
    "internal-or-service")
| where Verdict != "internal-or-service"
| summarize
    Writes          = count(),
    Verdicts        = make_set(Verdict, 5),
    ExternalSeen    = make_set(ExternalAccounts, 10),
    FederatedSeen   = make_set(FedStr, 5),
    ServicesSeen    = make_set(tostring(SvcP), 5),
    ConditionsSeen  = make_set(CondStr, 5),
    RolesTouched    = make_set(RoleName, 10),
    Events          = make_set(EventName, 3),
    CreatedRoleArns = make_set(tostring(Resp.role.arn), 5),
    SourceIPs       = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
    InvokedBy       = make_set(UserIdentityInvokedBy, 3),
    FirstSeen       = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen        = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserIdentityArn, bin(TimeGenerated, 10m)
| project FirstSeen, LastSeen, UserIdentityArn, Verdicts, RolesTouched, ExternalSeen,
          FederatedSeen, ConditionsSeen, ServicesSeen, CreatedRoleArns, Events,
          Writes, SourceIPs, InvokedBy
| order by LastSeen desc
//
// ============================================================================
// COMPANION — "WAS IT USED", the pivot that turns a config change into an intrusion
// ============================================================================
// Verified against the AWS-published cross-account example. When a principal in another
// account assumes a role here, THIS account's trail carries its own AssumeRole event, and
// its userIdentity has a shape nothing else produces:
//
//     "userIdentity": { "type": "AWSAccount",
//                       "principalId": "AIDA...",
//                       "accountId": "<the CALLING account>" }
//
// There is no userIdentity.arn on it. A query keyed on the ARN returns nothing and reads
// as "not used". The events are management events, so they are queryable everywhere
// management events are — this is not a data-plane blind spot.
//
// EVIDENCE LIMIT, stated because its absence is misread as safety: CloudTrail does not log
// DENIED cross-account assume-role requests in the target account. An empty result here is
// evidence about successful use only, never about attempts.
//
// THE ALLOWLIST IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. Without one, `UserIdentityType in
// ("WebIdentityUser","SAMLUser")` is every workforce SSO sign-in and every CI job in the
// estate, each carrying the P0 verdict. The provider identity is NOT in a flattened
// column — the AWSCloudTrail table publishes UserIdentityType, UserIdentityAccountId,
// UserIdentityArn, UserIdentityInvokedBy and the rest, but nothing for
// userIdentity.identityProvider — so it is read from the dynamic columns instead, and it
// lives in a different one per protocol:
//   SAML  requestParameters.principalArn  = arn:aws:iam::<acct>:saml-provider/<Name>
//   OIDC  responseElements.provider       = arn:aws:iam::<acct>:oidc-provider/<issuer>
// Both appear in AWS's own AssumeRoleWithSAML / AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity example events,
// and AWS states that for the three AssumeRole* APIs "CloudTrail will include the full
// responseElements except secretAccessKey", so the OIDC half is reliably present.
// userIdentity.identityProvider would NOT serve both: for SAMLUser it is the
// saml:namequalifier hash ("bdGOnTesti4+ExamplexL/jEvs=" in the AWS example), which no
// provider-ARN allowlist can ever match.
//
// let OrgAccounts   = dynamic(["<this-account-id>", "<other-org-account-id>"]);
// let KnownIdPRegex = @"(oidc-provider/<your-idp-host>|saml-provider/<YourIdPName>|cognito-identity\.amazonaws\.com)";
// AWSCloudTrail
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where EventSource == "sts.amazonaws.com"
// | where EventName in ("AssumeRole", "AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity", "AssumeRoleWithSAML")
// | where isempty(ErrorCode)
// | extend Req  = parse_json(RequestParameters)
// | extend Resp = parse_json(ResponseElements)
// | extend RoleArn  = tostring(Req.roleArn)
// // Join key back to the first query: the trust write logs roleName, the use logs roleArn.
// | extend RoleName = tostring(split(RoleArn, "/")[-1])
// | extend Session  = tostring(Req.roleSessionName)
// // UserIdentityType values documented for the flattened column do not include the two
// // federated forms, but AWS's own example events carry them. Match both and confirm the
// // literal strings against your trail.
// | where UserIdentityType == "AWSAccount"
//      or UserIdentityType in ("WebIdentityUser", "SAMLUser", "FederatedUser")
// | extend CallingAccount = tostring(UserIdentityAccountId)
// | extend IdPArn = coalesce(tostring(Req.principalArn), tostring(Resp.provider))
// | extend KnownIdP = isnotempty(IdPArn) and IdPArn matches regex KnownIdPRegex
// | extend External = UserIdentityType == "AWSAccount"
//                     and isnotempty(CallingAccount)
//                     and not(set_has_element(OrgAccounts, CallingAccount))
// // An empty IdPArn is NOT an allowlist miss — it is a federated event whose provider
// // this query could not read. Keep it, and disposition it against the role's live trust
// // document; do not let it pass as known.
// | where External
//      or (UserIdentityType in ("WebIdentityUser", "SAMLUser") and not(KnownIdP))
// | summarize Assumptions = count(),
//             CallingAccounts = make_set(CallingAccount, 5),
//             IdentityTypes   = make_set(UserIdentityType, 3),
//             IdPArns         = make_set(IdPArn, 5),
//             Sessions        = make_set(Session, 5),
//             SourceIPs       = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
//             SharedEventIds  = make_set(SharedEventId, 3),
//             FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
//             by RoleName, RoleArn
// | extend Verdict = strcat("ROLE ASSUMED FROM OUTSIDE THE ORGANISATION — correlate RoleName ",
//                            "against the trust writes above; a match within minutes is the ",
//                            "backdoor being exercised, P0")
// | order by LastSeen desc
//
// ============================================================================
// COMPANION — ATTEMPTED AND REJECTED SHAPES, the error path
// ============================================================================
// The four shapes a role trust policy refuses (NotPrincipal, bare "Principal":"*",
// "Federated":"*", "Service":"*") all fail with MalformedPolicyDocument. Nothing is
// stored, so no content rule can see them — but somebody wrote them, and a principal
// working through rejected principal shapes against a role it wants to reshape is the
// same actor as the AccessDenied probing. Keep this separate from the success path: an
// attempt and a completed rewrite are different incidents.
//
// AWSCloudTrail
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where EventSource == "iam.amazonaws.com"
// | where EventName in ("UpdateAssumeRolePolicy", "CreateRole")
// | where ErrorCode == "MalformedPolicyDocument"
// | extend Req = parse_json(RequestParameters)
// | extend DocRaw = tostring(coalesce(Req.policyDocument, Req.assumeRolePolicyDocument))
// // `contains`, never `has` — every operand here is punctuated.
// | extend Shape = case(
//     DocRaw contains "NotPrincipal",  "NotPrincipal in a role trust policy",
//     DocRaw contains "\"Federated\"", "Federated principal syntax",
//     DocRaw contains "\"Service\"",   "Service principal syntax",
//     "other malformed document")
// | summarize Attempts = count(), Shapes = make_set(Shape, 4),
//             Roles = make_set(tostring(Req.roleName), 10),
//             SourceIPs = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
//             FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
//             by UserIdentityArn
// | extend Verdict = strcat("REJECTED TRUST-POLICY SHAPES — an actor trying principal forms IAM ",
//                            "refuses. Correlate with the denied-write correlation; treat as ",
//                            "reconnaissance, P2")
// | order by LastSeen desc
//
// The SharedEventId is the same GUID in the calling account's trail and in this one. If
// the calling account is one you own, that GUID is how you attribute the session to a
// named principal there — this account's copy only ever names the account.
//
// ============================================================================
// FIELD SHAPE AND ERROR STRINGS — easy to get wrong, fails silently
// ============================================================================
// UpdateAssumeRolePolicy returns NO responseElements. Everything about the change is in
// requestParameters, and the PREVIOUS trust policy is nowhere: the call overwrites it.
// The only copy of what the trust used to be is the earlier CloudTrail event that set it.
//
// CreateRole NESTS its response: responseElements.role.arn, .roleName, .roleId,
// .assumeRolePolicyDocument — never a flat responseElements.arn. A flat path yields null
// silently, which is why CreatedRoleArns above reads Resp.role.arn.
//
// The trust document IS present on both sides of a CreateRole event —
// requestParameters.assumeRolePolicyDocument and responseElements.role.assumeRolePolicyDocument.
// AWS documents the RFC 3986 encoding for what GetRole returns, and documents nothing
// about the encoding of the CreateRole response field; the conditional decode above is
// correct for either, which is the reason it is conditional.
//
// Denials on this path: AccessDenied, and AccessDeniedException for service-evaluated
// denials — match the substring, then confirm the exact form against a real denied event.
// The complete non-denial set from the two API references: MalformedPolicyDocument,
// NoSuchEntity, LimitExceeded, ServiceFailure, and UnmodifiableEntity (service-linked
// roles, UpdateAssumeRolePolicy only) plus EntityAlreadyExists, InvalidInput and
// ConcurrentModification (CreateRole only). None is a denial and none may be counted as
// probing — but MalformedPolicyDocument is not inert either, see the companion above.
//
// ============================================================================
// PREVENTION
// ============================================================================
// An SCP denying iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy to everyone outside the IAM-administration
// role set is the only control that holds when detection is muted, and IAM Access
// Analyzer with the organization as its zone of trust is the only control that reports a
// role trusting an outside principal WITHOUT depending on having seen the write. Deploy
// both: the SCP stops the change, the analyzer catches what predates the SCP.
